// /install: getting blaze, trimmed from docs/install.md (the full version, with every detail, stays there).
site {
  install : Page {
    Title: {"Install"}
    Nav: {"Install", 1}
    Icon: {"download"}
    (InGroup, nav.start)
    Description: {"Install blaze with one line on macOS on Apple silicon or Linux, or from crates.io or Nix."}
    Intro: {"[warm] Installing blaze. It's one line, and you don't need Rust installed first."}
    Outro: {"[warm] That's it: one line, then blaze run. The usage page picks up from there. Thanks for listening."}

    Lede lede("One line, on macOS on Apple silicon or Linux, with or without Rust installed:") {
      Say: {"[warm] The short way: one line in your terminal. It works on macOS on Apple silicon and on Linux, whether or not you have Rust installed. It's the first command on this page."}
    }
    Command curl("curl -fsSL https://ix.dev/blaze | sh") {
      Silent
    }
    P then("Then, in a project of yours, `blaze run`. Change a function and run it again: the `Finished` line now says `[ra-jit]` (blaze's engine), which means blaze built it, not cargo. What to do next: [Usage](/usage).") {
      Say: {"[curious] Then go to one of your projects and type blaze run. Change a function and run it again. The finished line now says R A jit, that's blaze's engine, which means blaze built it, not cargo. [short pause] From there, the usage page says what to do next."}
    }
    Code first(lang: "bash", text: "blaze run")

    Section needs(title: "What you need") {
      Say: {"[matter-of-fact] What you need. A Mac with Apple silicon, or Linux on x86 or ARM, with a recent glibc: RHEL 9, Debian 12, Ubuntu 22.04, or anything newer. Intel Macs and musl systems like Alpine aren't supported. [short pause] A C compiler for linking. In a container, user namespaces, since build scripts and proc macros run in a sandbox made of them. And memory to spare: blaze's background process holds a few gigabytes for a small project. [pause] What you don't need is Rust. blaze is its own toolchain: it downloads its pinned nightly once, and builds every project with it, whatever the project pins."}
      Ul list {
        Li platform("**macOS on Apple silicon, or Linux on x86-64 or ARM64.** Intel Macs are not supported. Windows x86-64 (MSVC) builds through blaze from [crates.io](#crates), with no installer and no sandbox yet ([platforms](https://github.com/indexable-inc/blaze/blob/main/docs/platforms.md#windows-x86-64-msvc)).")
        Li glibc("**On Linux, glibc 2.34 or newer:** RHEL 9, Debian 12, Ubuntu 22.04 and anything newer. Alpine and other musl systems are not supported; the installer says so.")
        Li cc("**A C compiler for linking:** `gcc` or `cc` on Linux, Apple's command line tools on macOS (`xcode-select --install`).")
        Li container("**In a container, user namespaces.** Build scripts and proc macros run in a sandbox made of Linux namespaces, which Docker's default seccomp profile refuses: run the container with `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined`, or set `BLAZE_SANDBOX=off` to run them outside a sandbox, as cargo does.")
        Li memory("**Memory to spare.** blaze's background process holds a few GB for a small project and tens of GB for the largest ([numbers](https://github.com/indexable-inc/blaze/blob/main/docs/benchmarks.md#how-much-memory-and-disk-it-takes)).")
      }
      P rust("You do not need Rust or rustup. blaze is its own toolchain: it downloads its pinned nightly once into `~/.blaze` (rustc, cargo, the standard library and its source, about 100 to 130 MB), or uses rustup's copy of that nightly when it has one. It builds your project with that nightly whatever its `rust-toolchain.toml` says, so stable code and `#![feature(...)]` code both build ([why](https://github.com/indexable-inc/blaze/blob/main/docs/toolchain.md)).")
    }

    Section installer(title: "What the installer does") {
      Say: {"[calm] What the installer does. It downloads the build for your machine from blaze's GitHub releases, checks it against the published checksums, puts blaze and cargo blaze in your cargo bin directory, and has blaze fetch its Rust. It asks nothing. [short pause] Run it again to upgrade. Releases are dated, and a new one goes out whenever main is green and has changed."}
      P what("It downloads the build for your machine from the [releases](https://github.com/indexable-inc/blaze/releases), checks it against the published checksums, puts `blaze`, `cargo-blaze` and the programs they run in `~/.cargo/bin`, and has blaze download its Rust. It asks nothing. Run it again to upgrade.")
      P versions("Releases are dated, `0.YYYYMMDD.N`, and one is published whenever main is green and has changed. To install a given release, end the line with `| sh -s -- --version 0.YYYYMMDD.N`; `--help` says what else it takes. `cargo blaze <command>` is the same as `blaze <command>`.")
    }

    Section crates(title: "From crates.io") {
      Say: {"[matter-of-fact] You can also build blaze from source with cargo, from crates dot io, using the nightly blaze is pinned to. It takes a few minutes. Or use cargo binstall to fetch a release's build instead of compiling it."}
      Code cargo(lang: "bash", text: "cargo +nightly-2026-10-27 install --locked cargo-blaze")
      P what("This compiles blaze from source with the nightly it is pinned to, which takes a few minutes. The pin is in [harness/toolchain](https://github.com/indexable-inc/blaze/blob/main/harness/toolchain) and moves to the newest nightly most days; `rustup toolchain install` it first. It puts its programs in `~/.cargo/bin`; keep them together, they find each other there. `cargo binstall cargo-blaze` installs a release's build instead.")
    }

    Section nix(title: "With Nix") {
      Say: {"[matter-of-fact] And with Nix: install it into your profile from the GitHub flake, or try it without installing anything with nix run, which is the same as cargo blaze build. Nix compiles blaze from source; there's no binary cache yet."}
      Code profile(lang: "bash", text: `nix profile install github:indexable-inc/blaze#blaze
        nix run github:indexable-inc/blaze#blaze -- build   # try it without installing`)
      Dim cache("Nix compiles blaze from source; there is no binary cache yet. In a flake, take `blaze.packages.\$\{system}.blaze` from the input `github:indexable-inc/blaze`.")
    }

    Section old_name(title: "The old name") {
      Say: {"[light] One last thing. blaze was called R A jit until October twenty-fourth, twenty twenty-six, and cargo R A jit still works as another name for cargo blaze."}
      P what("blaze was called ra-jit until 2026-10-24. `cargo ra-jit` keeps working as another name for `cargo blaze`, and its settings work as `BLAZE_*` and as `RA_JIT_*`. Every way to build blaze itself: [contributing](https://github.com/indexable-inc/blaze/blob/main/docs/contributing.md).")
    }
  }
}
